WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.476 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.476 --> 00:00:03.444 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:05.904 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:10.862 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.862 --> 00:00:12.320 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Well, today we're

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.120 align:middle line:84%
going to talk about
volatility modeling.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.800 align:middle line:84%
And this topic in finance
is one that has really

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.240 align:middle line:90%
interested me for decades.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.920 align:middle line:84%
And there are a lot of
subtle details involved

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.340 align:middle line:84%
with understanding
volatility, how to measure it,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.880 align:middle line:90%
how to forecast it.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And we've had some talks
already with the group projects

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.680 align:middle line:84%
about dealing with
volatility estimation

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.240 align:middle line:84%
and measuring volatility, in
particular implied volatility.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.760 align:middle line:84%
Today we'll focus more on
realized volatility and modeling

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.920 align:middle line:90%
that.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.530 align:middle line:84%
And so to begin, here's just
an overview of different ways

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.349 align:middle line:90%
one might define volatility.

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:08.790 align:middle line:84%
And its basic definition is the
annualized standard deviation

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.690 align:middle line:84%
of the change in price or
value of a financial security.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.790 align:middle line:84%
And of course, this
concept is really

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:23.990 align:middle line:84%
important for measuring risk and
evaluating the value of options

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.370 align:middle line:90%
on underlying assets.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:26.163 align:middle line:90%
Yep?

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:28.227 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What do you
mean by annualized?

00:01:28.227 --> 00:01:29.810 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
What that means, OK,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:43.910 align:middle line:84%
suppose we have a sigma day
that's equal to, say, 0.01%.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.190 align:middle line:90%
So 1% variability per day.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.630 align:middle line:84%
If we wanted to measure
volatility on an annualized

00:01:52.630 --> 00:02:00.050 align:middle line:84%
scale, then we could consider
possibly the square root

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:04.650 align:middle line:90%
of 252 times sigma day.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's a matter of
scaling the volatility up

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.289 align:middle line:90%
to an annual horizon.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.410 align:middle line:84%
And the value of
that rescaling is

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.610 align:middle line:84%
that we can talk
about volatilities

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.730 align:middle line:90%
over different maturities.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.970 align:middle line:84%
And they're measured
on the same scale.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.890 align:middle line:84%
And with different
estimation approaches,

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.050 align:middle line:84%
there's historical sample
volatility measures

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:39.690 align:middle line:84%
which don't require really any
probability models with them.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.590 align:middle line:84%
Then we have geometric
Brownian motion.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.130 align:middle line:84%
That's a very familiar
model to everyone here.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.250 align:middle line:84%
And we can consider
extensions of that

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.490 align:middle line:84%
to the Poisson jump
diffusion model.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:57.200 align:middle line:84%
A group delivered
a nice lecture note

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.600 align:middle line:90%
on the Poisson jump diffusions.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.440 align:middle line:84%
And then following
these cases are

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.960 align:middle line:84%
cases where volatility might
have some time dynamics in them.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.400 align:middle line:84%
With these geometric Brownian
motion and Poisson jump

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.280 align:middle line:84%
diffusion models, we
think of volatility

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.640 align:middle line:84%
being some constant,
which is the same from one

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.480 align:middle line:90%
period to the next.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.280 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out
that there can be

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.600 align:middle line:90%
time dependence in volatility.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.240 align:middle line:84%
And so there are ARCH
and GARCH models,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.520 align:middle line:84%
which stand for Autoregressive
Conditional Heteroskedastic

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:39.680 align:middle line:90%
Models.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.200 align:middle line:84%
And then the GARCH are
generalized versions of those.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.960 align:middle line:84%
And then there are stochastic
volatility models as well.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.260 align:middle line:84%
I know, let's see, the
Heston option pricing model

00:03:54.260 --> 00:04:01.040 align:middle line:84%
uses a stochastic volatility
term in its formulation.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.640 align:middle line:84%
So we have those
kinds of extensions.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.740 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, there's
implied volatility

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.060 align:middle line:90%
from options and derivatives.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's just look at
computing volatility

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.820 align:middle line:84%
from an historical
series of actual prices.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:26.300 align:middle line:84%
If we have prices PT, then
we can define the log returns

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.900 align:middle line:84%
by looking at the ratio
of prices from one day

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.820 align:middle line:84%
to the next, or one
period to the next,

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.220 align:middle line:84%
and taking the
logarithm of that.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.780 align:middle line:84%
This is a useful way
of measuring returns

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.980 align:middle line:84%
as an alternative to simply the
percentage change in value using

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:52.210 align:middle line:84%
the log scale or the
log return, if we

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:57.730 align:middle line:84%
look at the cumulative change
over successive periods.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:01.210 align:middle line:90%
With log returns, they just add.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.530 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if we were using
percentage returns,

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:08.090 align:middle line:84%
we'd have to basically multiply
factors together representing

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:08.850 align:middle line:90%
that.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:16.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, in modeling
volatility, we consider

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:22.650 align:middle line:84%
models that correspond
to stationary processes

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.770 align:middle line:90%
for our returns.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:29.730 align:middle line:84%
So the log returns
of price series

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:36.690 align:middle line:84%
are often consistent via tests
with a stationary distribution

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.250 align:middle line:90%
of returns.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.930 align:middle line:84%
And under the assumption
of covariance stationarity,

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:48.010 align:middle line:84%
we can define sigma
to be the variance of

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.850 align:middle line:84%
or the standard deviation
of those returns.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:57.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, in this notation here,
this sigma for the volatility

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.510 align:middle line:84%
would be corresponding to a
frequency of observation that's

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.550 align:middle line:90%
daily or weekly or monthly.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.030 align:middle line:84%
And we'd have sample
estimates given

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:15.910 align:middle line:84%
by the square root of the sample
variance where we traditionally

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:19.430 align:middle line:84%
use the estimator
for variance, which

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.130 align:middle line:84%
is an unbiased estimate
of the variance.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:28.950 align:middle line:84%
So that factor divisor t
minus 1 is included there.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.390 align:middle line:84%
And then if we want to
annualize these values,

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:38.430 align:middle line:84%
it's common practice just
to treat these returns

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:44.210 align:middle line:84%
as independent or
uncorrelated over time,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:49.580 align:middle line:84%
so that the variance of a sum
is the sum of the variances.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:54.240 align:middle line:84%
So there are roughly
252 days in a year.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:02.000 align:middle line:84%
So we multiply by the square
root of 252 for daily returns.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.420 align:middle line:84%
For weekly returns, we'd
do the root 52 times

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.260 align:middle line:84%
sigma hat and route
12 for monthly.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:20.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, when measuring
historical volatility,

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:24.660 align:middle line:84%
we can think of a simple
historical average

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.180 align:middle line:84%
since the beginning
of our time series.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.180 align:middle line:84%
So this historical average
would use all available data.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:37.420 align:middle line:84%
It's reasonable to think that
volatility isn't necessarily

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:42.820 align:middle line:84%
stable over the entire time
range of a price series.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:48.350 align:middle line:84%
And so we could consider
just using the last m values.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:51.970 align:middle line:84%
So take a rolling
average of the last m

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:58.950 align:middle line:84%
values of this sigma
hat squared t minus j.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:06.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we define
sigma hat squared t

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:11.230 align:middle line:84%
to just be a single
day's volatility,

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:18.730 align:middle line:84%
that could be like the square of
the daily return on a given day.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:27.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, using equal weighted
values over a window of length m

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:33.490 align:middle line:84%
might be improved upon by using
an exponential moving average.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:36.330 align:middle line:84%
And so exponential
moving averages

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:41.610 align:middle line:84%
are averages where we
take a weighted average

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:47.990 align:middle line:84%
of the previous
estimate of volatility

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:51.950 align:middle line:84%
and the current
estimate of volatility

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.530 align:middle line:90%
using the time t value.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.710 align:middle line:84%
So this 1 minus beta
beta weight allows

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:06.970 align:middle line:84%
us to weight the past versus the
present in an exponential way.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:11.210 align:middle line:84%
And with this formula,
it's recursive over t.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:15.070 align:middle line:84%
And so it can actually
apply across an entire range

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:17.590 align:middle line:90%
of historical data.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:22.190 align:middle line:84%
An alternative to just the
regular exponential moving

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.310 align:middle line:84%
averages and exponential
weighted moving

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:29.190 align:middle line:84%
average where we only
go back maybe m periods,

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:36.590 align:middle line:84%
but we decrease the weights
on past volatility estimates

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:40.010 align:middle line:84%
according to the
power of beta j.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:47.220 align:middle line:84%
So beta j or beta
is a decay factor

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.060 align:middle line:90%
in estimating volatilities.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.400 align:middle line:90%


00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.820 align:middle line:84%
Additionally, one could
consider regression models

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:06.180 align:middle line:84%
with these volatility
sigma hats indexed by t.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:12.860 align:middle line:84%
And depending on
the relationship

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:18.180 align:middle line:84%
of volatility to the past,
we might use more data

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.120 align:middle line:84%
to increase precision
of estimators,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:24.700 align:middle line:84%
but use data closer
to time t when

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:28.820 align:middle line:84%
we believe that the volatility
may be changing over time.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:32.980 align:middle line:84%
And what's neat about
these estimation

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:39.360 align:middle line:84%
methods is that we can evaluate,
say, the exponential moving

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.000 align:middle line:84%
average estimates of volatility
over different assets

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.680 align:middle line:90%
and different asset classes.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:56.480 align:middle line:84%
And this approach was
applied in RiskMetrics.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:03.360 align:middle line:84%
Here there's a web link to the
RiskMetrics technical document.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.450 align:middle line:90%
I think I have it listed here.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:08.400 align:middle line:90%


00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:17.600 align:middle line:84%
So actually this RiskMetrics
was created by JP Morgan back

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.840 align:middle line:90%
in the 1990s.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.400 align:middle line:84%
Coincidentally, when I was
at the Sloan School teaching

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.320 align:middle line:84%
statistics and
financial modeling,

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.120 align:middle line:84%
I was part of the Financial
Services Research Center

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.240 align:middle line:84%
that was sponsored by JP
Morgan and other groups

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:41.190 align:middle line:84%
when this risk metrics
document was created.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:45.030 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at,
let's see, there's

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:53.390 align:middle line:84%
a section here of risk metrics
decay factors for volatility.

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:56.790 align:middle line:84%
Basically, for
different countries,

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:04.270 align:middle line:84%
one can use the exponential
moving average of asset returns

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:08.710 align:middle line:84%
for foreign exchange
10 year 0 prices equity

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:11.750 align:middle line:90%
indices and other assets.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:16.390 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting to
see is that the decay factor

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.290 align:middle line:90%
is generally well above 0.9.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:24.110 align:middle line:84%
It's more like 0.95, which
suggests that volatility

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.110 align:middle line:90%
is changing slowly over time.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:31.790 align:middle line:84%
And the way they chose
these decay factors was

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:38.090 align:middle line:84%
to look at how predictive they
were with mean squared error

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.110 align:middle line:90%
of volatility.

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:44.230 align:middle line:84%
So these are reasonable
estimates of volatility.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:49.450 align:middle line:84%
And an important aspect
of these measures

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:55.290 align:middle line:84%
is having benchmark values
that people can agree upon.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So we're not arguing that
these are the best estimators,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:05.370 align:middle line:84%
but they're a simple objective
estimator that can be applied.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:08.650 align:middle line:84%
And when we communicate
about these other people,

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:10.890 align:middle line:90%
understand what we're measuring.

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.930 align:middle line:90%
So those are important.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:17.530 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:21.050 align:middle line:84%
With geometric Brownian
motion, turning now

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:28.130 align:middle line:84%
to having a stochastic
process model for the prices.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:31.330 align:middle line:84%
If we have this
stochastic differential

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:37.240 align:middle line:84%
equation, the geometric Brownian
motion one, we see that,

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:42.840 align:middle line:84%
and it's totally obvious, but if
we divide both sides by s of t,

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:55.560 align:middle line:84%
we get mu dt plus sigma ds of t
or sigma dw of t, where w of t

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.980 align:middle line:90%
is a standard Brownian motion.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:03.960 align:middle line:84%
So by looking at
essentially the percentage

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.880 align:middle line:84%
change on an
infinitesimal level,

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:13.160 align:middle line:84%
we basically have a linear
trend that's deterministic

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:16.880 align:middle line:84%
and then a random walk
with Brownian motion

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:20.280 align:middle line:90%
steps on that scale.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And with this geometric
Brownian motion,

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:32.900 align:middle line:84%
we have basically independent
increments with normally

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:38.660 align:middle line:84%
distributed increments on this
log scale for the Brownian

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:40.100 align:middle line:90%
motion.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And with our homework exercise
this past week, we actually

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:54.820 align:middle line:84%
dealt with estimating the drift
parameter and the volatility

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:58.300 align:middle line:84%
parameter in geometric
Brownian motion.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:05.100 align:middle line:84%
And so if our increments of
time are unit increments,

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:11.420 align:middle line:84%
then we can estimate
a drift term mu star

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:14.300 align:middle line:90%
with the average log return.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:22.300 align:middle line:84%
And we can estimate the period
volatility or period variance

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:28.300 align:middle line:84%
with the sample variance
using n instead of n minus 1.

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:33.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, these essentially
correspond to maximum likelihood

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:35.850 align:middle line:90%
estimates of these parameters.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:41.450 align:middle line:84%
And what's important
to note here

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:51.170 align:middle line:84%
is that the drift term
mu star per period

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:55.810 align:middle line:84%
is actually equal to the mu
of the geometric Brownian

00:15:55.810 --> 00:16:02.450 align:middle line:84%
motion minus the square of the
volatility term divided by 2.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:05.730 align:middle line:84%
And so what's
important is that when

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:13.290 align:middle line:84%
we apply this stochastic
differential equation,

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:18.330 align:middle line:84%
there basically
comes an extra term

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:22.890 align:middle line:84%
in the drift that's due
to the squared variation

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.810 align:middle line:90%
in the process.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:29.200 align:middle line:84%
So in working with these
the stochastic differential

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:34.260 align:middle line:84%
equations, it's important not to
just look at first order terms,

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:37.680 align:middle line:84%
but to look at second order
terms in your Taylor series

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:38.540 align:middle line:90%
approximation.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:41.960 align:middle line:84%
And that's how that
mu star gets in there

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.160 align:middle line:90%
to be different from mu.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:50.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, an interesting exercise,
which hopefully was of interest

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:53.920 align:middle line:84%
to people who worked
on that last week,

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:59.400 align:middle line:84%
if we vary the increments
delta j over which we observe

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:10.119 align:middle line:84%
this price process, then we
can talk about how accurate

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:18.200 align:middle line:84%
or how precise are our estimates
of mu star and sigma squared.

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:25.780 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if
the delta j, the increments

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:34.260 align:middle line:84%
between observations varies,
then our estimate of mu star

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.020 align:middle line:90%
is always the same.

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:39.580 align:middle line:84%
It corresponds to just
the cumulative return

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:43.240 align:middle line:84%
over the entire period divided
by the length of the period.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:47.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's the average rate
of return per period.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:53.180 align:middle line:84%
Interestingly, if we increase
the number of increments,

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:57.140 align:middle line:84%
then our precision
on the variance

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:03.100 align:middle line:84%
gets more and more precise,
and the variability of that

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:07.260 align:middle line:84%
actually goes down
with a factor of n.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.580 align:middle line:84%
So that was a homework
exercise that I think

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:16.700 align:middle line:90%
is an important one to realize.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:22.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, a really
interesting extension

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:27.370 align:middle line:84%
of maximum likelihood
estimation with normally

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:32.490 align:middle line:84%
distributed increments coming
from the Brownian motion process

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:39.850 align:middle line:84%
is looking at the problem of
using more information than just

00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:43.130 align:middle line:90%
the close to close prices.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:47.770 align:middle line:84%
And so what we want
to do is introduce you

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.610 align:middle line:90%
to the Garman-Klass estimator.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:59.490 align:middle line:84%
And let me just turn to
the Garman-Klass paper.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:04.210 align:middle line:84%
These papers are distributed in
Canvas in the volatility notes

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:05.490 align:middle line:90%
section.

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:12.450 align:middle line:84%
But if we think of a period
of time with one day,

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:19.650 align:middle line:84%
going from yesterday's close to
today's open to today's close,

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:27.070 align:middle line:84%
then in thinking about
measuring volatility

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:30.710 align:middle line:84%
using the difference
between today's close

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:35.390 align:middle line:84%
and yesterday's close,
that can be very useful,

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:38.590 align:middle line:84%
but it's missing out
on other information

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:42.830 align:middle line:84%
that we may have access
to, such as what's

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:47.910 align:middle line:90%
the opening price today?

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:49.810 align:middle line:90%
What's the low value today?

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:51.710 align:middle line:90%
What's the high value today?

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:55.150 align:middle line:84%
And how can we use
that extra information

00:19:55.150 --> 00:20:00.910 align:middle line:84%
to have more precise
estimates of volatility?

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:06.350 align:middle line:84%
Anecdotally, when I was studying
for a doctorate in statistics

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:13.710 align:middle line:84%
at Berkeley, I was a research
assistant to Michael Klass.

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:18.310 align:middle line:84%
And I was getting interested
in non-statistics topics

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:22.580 align:middle line:84%
like finance, and so I took a
course from Mark Garman, who

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:24.840 align:middle line:90%
co-wrote this paper.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:29.160 align:middle line:84%
So it's rather interesting
to have had that exposure.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:33.500 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it was a very
inspiring set of classes

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:38.980 align:middle line:84%
that I took, which motivated
my, I guess, lifelong interest

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.980 align:middle line:90%
in financial modeling.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:45.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look
at some notation.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:54.020 align:middle line:84%
Very simply, we'll think of just
a single period, like one day,

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:59.260 align:middle line:84%
and refer to the
closing price on day j,

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:06.540 align:middle line:84%
opening price on day j, the
high and low on day j as well.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:12.580 align:middle line:84%
Importantly, the notation
here includes this--

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:15.580 align:middle line:84%
well, includes the time
points, but it also

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:23.960 align:middle line:84%
includes the parameter f,
which is the length of time

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:26.360 align:middle line:84%
that the market is
closed on a given day.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:32.000 align:middle line:84%
So the time between the close
and open of the next day.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.200 align:middle line:90%
So we have this notation.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:41.360 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider just
focusing on single periods,

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:44.960 align:middle line:84%
and in this case,
the first one, if we

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:48.560 align:middle line:84%
look at the change
in closing values,

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:51.320 align:middle line:84%
now, perhaps it makes
sense to think of these

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:55.760 align:middle line:90%
on the log scale of the prices.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:02.040 align:middle line:84%
The close to close
squared return

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:07.760 align:middle line:84%
basically will have
mean sigma squared

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:11.840 align:middle line:84%
and variance equal to
2 sigma to the fourth.

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:16.310 align:middle line:84%
Now, this corresponds
to the theory

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:21.910 align:middle line:84%
of normal random variables
for returns, which

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:23.910 align:middle line:90%
comes from the Brownian motion.

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:31.310 align:middle line:84%
And the expected
squared value is--

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:35.150 align:middle line:84%
well, actually, we can think
of maybe the mean return

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:41.270 align:middle line:84%
being 0 or being subtracted
from all the returns.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:45.750 align:middle line:84%
But the variance of
this sigma 0 hat squared

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:49.030 align:middle line:84%
is the variance of a chi
squared random variable

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:50.990 align:middle line:90%
with 1 degree of freedom.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:53.590 align:middle line:84%
And so in probability
classes, we

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:58.670 align:middle line:84%
learn that a chi squared
random variable has expectation

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:00.790 align:middle line:90%
equal to the degrees of freedom.

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:03.190 align:middle line:84%
And if it's a
scaled chi squared,

00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:06.910 align:middle line:84%
it's the scale factor
sigma squared times that.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:09.110 align:middle line:84%
And the variance
of a chi squared

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:11.590 align:middle line:84%
is equal to twice the
degrees of freedom.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:14.980 align:middle line:84%
So that's where that
formula comes from.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:20.700 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we look at the close
to open squared return,

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:25.540 align:middle line:84%
the same arguments lead to
this sigma 1 hat squared.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:30.780 align:middle line:84%
Looking at the squared
return from closed to open,

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:37.820 align:middle line:84%
normalized by the length of
the period between the close

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:42.340 align:middle line:84%
and open, this will
also have expectation

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:44.500 align:middle line:90%
equal to sigma squared.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:49.500 align:middle line:84%
And its variance will also
be 2 sigma to the fourth.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:56.180 align:middle line:84%
So if the model applies
throughout the time

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:59.820 align:middle line:84%
periods of the market
being open as well

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:04.660 align:middle line:84%
as the market being closed,
then these formulas apply.

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:10.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, we could also do the
open to close squared return

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:18.880 align:middle line:84%
and, again, if we normalize the
squared change in price or log

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:23.400 align:middle line:84%
price, if we normalize that
by the length of the period

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:28.360 align:middle line:84%
1 minus f, then we get
the same properties

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:31.960 align:middle line:84%
of the moments for these
estimates of sigma squared.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:36.040 align:middle line:84%
So all of these have
expectation equal to sigma

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:41.560 align:middle line:84%
squared, and all of them have
the same degree of variation.

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:50.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, this second or the
index one and two cases

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:54.800 align:middle line:84%
are estimates that
use different time

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:58.480 align:middle line:84%
periods, different increments,
disjoint increments

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:00.720 align:middle line:90%
of the process.

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:05.640 align:middle line:84%
And so these have to be
independent if our Brownian

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:09.000 align:middle line:90%
motion model applies.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:15.750 align:middle line:84%
And so if we simply take the
weighted average of these

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:23.390 align:middle line:84%
or the simple average, then
the expectation is unchanged

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:29.470 align:middle line:84%
and the variance is equal to
basically 1/2 squared times

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:31.310 align:middle line:90%
the sum of the variances.

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:34.470 align:middle line:84%
And that equals actually
sigma to the fourth.

00:25:34.470 --> 00:25:38.710 align:middle line:84%
So we're able to get a
more precise estimate

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:45.710 align:middle line:84%
of the volatility using these
two increments separately.

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:52.710 align:middle line:84%
And so it's common in
statistical theory of estimators

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:58.710 align:middle line:84%
to talk about the efficiency of
one estimator versus another.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:06.390 align:middle line:84%
And so if we compare
this sigma star,

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:11.250 align:middle line:84%
we can divide its variance into
the variance of sigma 0 squared

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:13.410 align:middle line:90%
and get a factor of 2.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:16.180 align:middle line:90%
So we basically have--

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:18.890 align:middle line:90%


00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:19.390 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:20.440 align:middle line:90%
Have I got that correct?

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:23.662 align:middle line:90%


00:26:23.662 --> 00:26:24.610 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:29.490 align:middle line:84%
We basically have twice the
efficiency in this case.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:34.090 align:middle line:84%
So we're thinking of inverse
relationships of the variance.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:36.690 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Parkinson came up
with considering

00:26:43.370 --> 00:26:47.890 align:middle line:84%
the high and low
values over a period

00:26:47.890 --> 00:26:55.890 align:middle line:84%
and showed that this
estimate sigma hat 3 squared

00:26:55.890 --> 00:27:04.450 align:middle line:84%
is an unbiased estimate of sigma
squared when f is equal to 0

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:09.600 align:middle line:90%
and has efficiency equal to 5.2.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:17.400 align:middle line:84%
So this high and low data really
has a lot of information in it

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:22.720 align:middle line:84%
compared with just the
close to close returns.

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:26.000 align:middle line:84%
Our precision with estimating
sigma squared basically

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:30.600 align:middle line:84%
can increase by a
factor of 5 with using

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:34.130 align:middle line:84%
the high and low values
instead of the close to close.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:36.840 align:middle line:90%


00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:38.160 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:42.440 align:middle line:84%
I just posted today the
problem set due next week

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:48.760 align:middle line:84%
where there's an article by
Parkinson discussing issues

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:57.400 align:middle line:84%
of high and low or extreme value
estimates of the variance, which

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.060 align:middle line:84%
I think will be
interesting to read.

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:02.000 align:middle line:84%
I think it's
somewhat fun to read

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.900 align:middle line:84%
the classic papers
in these areas,

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:06.920 align:middle line:84%
especially because
the older they are,

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:08.840 align:middle line:84%
the easier they are
to read, I think,

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:11.900 align:middle line:84%
although sometimes
that's not totally true.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:17.260 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what Garman
and Klass did was

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:22.100 align:middle line:84%
they considered
alternatives, which

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:28.020 align:middle line:84%
included the high-low
volatility estimate.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:34.940 align:middle line:84%
And a simple average, or
weighted by a and 1 minus

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:42.380 align:middle line:84%
of this high-low estimate of
Parkinson and the sigma 1,

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:46.080 align:middle line:90%
which is the--

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:46.580 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:47.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's go back.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:54.180 align:middle line:84%
And the sigma 1, which is
the open to close value.

00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:59.660 align:middle line:84%
This has an even higher
efficiency of 6.2

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:04.790 align:middle line:84%
and a rather striking
result of their paper,

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:10.370 align:middle line:84%
as they were able to derive
the best analytic scale

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.810 align:middle line:90%
invariant estimator.

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:15.850 align:middle line:84%
So, depending on whether
you like mathematics or not,

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:18.370 align:middle line:90%
you may like this paper or not.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:21.210 align:middle line:84%
But it's really
interesting to see

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:27.050 align:middle line:84%
how a property of
finding a scale invariant

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:36.290 align:middle line:84%
estimator that is best among the
class of such estimators, that's

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:38.990 align:middle line:84%
actually derived by
Garman and Klass.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:48.410 align:middle line:84%
So let me just highlight how
this section five in their paper

00:29:48.410 --> 00:29:52.370 align:middle line:84%
talks about best analytic
scale invariant estimators.

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:57.170 align:middle line:84%
And you may be able to see
from the derivation here

00:29:57.170 --> 00:29:59.770 align:middle line:84%
that their arguments
just are dealing

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:03.840 align:middle line:84%
with some quadratic
forms and solving

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:07.600 align:middle line:84%
for properties, minimizing
these quadratic forms.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.160 align:middle line:84%
And so it's rather
neat that they're

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:14.080 align:middle line:84%
able to achieve
this result. Yeah?

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:16.872 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why is it
necessary [INAUDIBLE]?

00:30:16.872 --> 00:30:21.286 align:middle line:84%
Because that's just commenting
about the prepared bearings,

00:30:21.286 --> 00:30:21.786 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:30:21.786 --> 00:30:28.680 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Well, with
a higher efficient estimator,

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:34.400 align:middle line:84%
you can have as precise an
estimator with a smaller

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:37.520 align:middle line:90%
or shorter time series.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:42.040 align:middle line:84%
So instead of using, say,
20 days close to close,

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:46.920 align:middle line:84%
you could use a week of
data and have estimators

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.760 align:middle line:90%
that are comparably precise.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:51.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:53.280 align:middle line:90%


00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:55.798 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yeah, yeah.

00:30:55.798 --> 00:30:56.340 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.220 align:middle line:84%
And so there are all
these extra assumptions

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.720 align:middle line:90%
that complicate things.

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.660 align:middle line:84%
And in many of
these developments,

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:06.860 align:middle line:84%
they're simplifying
assumptions like the mean is 0.

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:13.140 align:middle line:84%
But if you were trying to model
time varying volatility, then

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:17.100 align:middle line:84%
rather than looking at, say,
close to close volatility

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.660 align:middle line:84%
and how that changes
over time, if you

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:26.100 align:middle line:84%
use these other estimators,
the Garman-Klass, for example,

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:29.140 align:middle line:84%
you could maybe use a
much shorter time period

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:33.100 align:middle line:84%
and be able to track or model
changes in volatility over time

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:33.880 align:middle line:90%
with that.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:39.300 align:middle line:90%


00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:40.160 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:46.900 align:middle line:90%


00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:49.960 align:middle line:84%
So then finally, there's
this composite estimator,

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:53.940 align:middle line:90%
which has efficiency 8.4.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:57.250 align:middle line:84%
And finally, there are
a number of extensions

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:02.010 align:middle line:84%
of the Garman-Klass
that are listed here.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:07.570 align:middle line:84%
And the one that is actually
recommended these days

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:12.150 align:middle line:84%
is actually this
Yang Zhang model,

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:16.370 align:middle line:84%
which has a drift independent
volatility estimation based

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:18.590 align:middle line:90%
on the high-low close.

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that it's
drift independent deals

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:27.090 align:middle line:84%
with that assumption
that was just mentioned.

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:29.170 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:35.530 align:middle line:84%
There's a case study
of using R to calculate

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:39.050 align:middle line:84%
these different
volatility estimates.

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:44.250 align:middle line:90%
And let's see here.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:52.210 align:middle line:84%
So here's the case
study, which goes

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:58.530 align:middle line:84%
through calculating different
measures of volatility,

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:02.030 align:middle line:84%
using close to close
Parkinson's all the way down

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:05.710 align:middle line:84%
to Yang and Zhang in section
1.6 and then comparing

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:10.670 align:middle line:90%
those for the S&P 500 Index.

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And we'll go through
these in a moment.

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:20.270 align:middle line:84%
But following just looking
at these individually,

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:25.270 align:middle line:84%
we consider time series modeling
of these different volatility

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:27.790 align:middle line:90%
statistics.

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:33.870 align:middle line:84%
And so in terms of understanding
realized volatility,

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:37.830 align:middle line:84%
a useful analysis is
to be able to predict

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:42.710 align:middle line:84%
how our realized volatility is
going to change into the future.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:45.390 align:middle line:84%
So there may be some
predictability there.

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:49.870 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see is
that in the time series

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:55.460 align:middle line:84%
analysis of these volatilities,
time series models

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:58.860 align:middle line:84%
such as autoregressive
integrated moving average models

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:03.900 align:middle line:84%
can be applied and
can be very useful.

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:07.540 align:middle line:84%
And in particular,
there's a role

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:12.300 align:middle line:84%
to be played with
seasonal ARIMA models.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.690 align:middle line:90%
So let's take a look at that.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:18.460 align:middle line:90%


00:34:18.460 --> 00:34:21.620 align:middle line:84%
And just as a
preview, let's see,

00:34:21.620 --> 00:34:26.060 align:middle line:84%
one of the problems in
the next problem set

00:34:26.060 --> 00:34:34.980 align:middle line:84%
is to look at this same set
of analysis for the S&P 500,

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:40.540 align:middle line:84%
look at the same analysis
for the stock ARKK, which

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:45.300 align:middle line:84%
is Cathie Wood's
ETF, and just see

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:48.420 align:middle line:90%
how different the results are.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:55.360 align:middle line:84%
And what's nice is that
the R markdown files

00:34:55.360 --> 00:35:00.280 align:middle line:84%
that create this case study can
be applied to any other stocks

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:03.400 align:middle line:84%
you want to analyze,
if you so choose.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:09.960 align:middle line:84%
For the problem set, I posted
the two versions of this study

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:13.000 align:middle line:90%
for S&P 500 and ARKK.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:15.100 align:middle line:84%
So you don't need
to do R at all.

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:23.060 align:middle line:84%
But if you set up a Posit Cloud
account with RStudio Cloud,

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:26.960 align:middle line:84%
you should be able to just
upload the file, the R markdown

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:31.800 align:middle line:84%
file, and rerun the programs
and do so with another stock,

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.720 align:middle line:90%
if you so choose.

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:36.840 align:middle line:84%
So in this note,
the first section

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:43.320 align:middle line:84%
just goes through and
looks at the S&P 500 index.

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:49.550 align:middle line:84%
It actually is going through
a couple of days ago.

00:35:49.550 --> 00:35:55.230 align:middle line:84%
It's a good time to
have an equity exposure.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:36:00.590 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at close
to close volatility,

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:06.590 align:middle line:84%
this is a graph of the
21 day variance scaled up

00:36:06.590 --> 00:36:13.310 align:middle line:84%
to an annualized basis by
multiplying by root 252.

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And what's important is to
see that volatility generally

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:25.150 align:middle line:90%
is well below 25%.

00:36:25.150 --> 00:36:32.190 align:middle line:84%
Often it's around 12%
to 15% when it's low,

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:33.990 align:middle line:90%
but it does have spikes.

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:38.790 align:middle line:84%
And during COVID, there
was the huge spike up.

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:46.970 align:middle line:84%
And more recently, there
was some elevated volatility

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:52.530 align:middle line:84%
that suggests that
volatility is not constant.

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:55.810 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, this is the
close to close volatility.

00:36:55.810 --> 00:37:01.650 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the high-low
volatility of Parkinson,

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:04.690 align:middle line:84%
then we just are
averaging the log

00:37:04.690 --> 00:37:10.570 align:middle line:84%
of the ratio of the high
to low and scaling it up.

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:15.690 align:middle line:84%
And so we get this plot,
which is very, very similar.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:20.930 align:middle line:84%
In principle, this
estimator is more precise,

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:25.010 align:middle line:84%
at least if our underlying
Brownian motion model

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:29.170 align:middle line:84%
or geometric Brownian
motion model is true,

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:31.810 align:middle line:84%
but it gives us
different values.

00:37:31.810 --> 00:37:36.770 align:middle line:84%
And then the Garman-Klass
model is here.

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:39.530 align:middle line:84%
So what's useful just
in this case study

00:37:39.530 --> 00:37:43.930 align:middle line:84%
is partly just seeing what the
formulas are for the volatility

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:48.160 align:middle line:84%
estimates and then being
able to compare those.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:53.240 align:middle line:84%
And then finally,
this Yang-Zhang model

00:37:53.240 --> 00:37:56.320 align:middle line:90%
is an extension of Garman-Klass.

00:37:56.320 --> 00:38:01.280 align:middle line:84%
And as it says here, this is
currently the preferred version

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:02.740 align:middle line:90%
of volatility estimates.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:09.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, in looking at these
different volatility estimates,

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:16.980 align:middle line:84%
we can create a panel series
of all the volatility measures.

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:20.320 align:middle line:84%
This shows, not
surprisingly, there's

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:24.040 align:middle line:84%
peaks at the same times
with all of these.

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:25.840 align:middle line:84%
What's more
interesting is to look

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:30.000 align:middle line:90%
how they move simultaneously.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.200 align:middle line:84%
And if you look
at too much data,

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:35.980 align:middle line:84%
it's hard to see
exactly what's going on.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So there's a graph
here of looking

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:43.380 align:middle line:84%
at just the last year,
almost two years of data,

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:46.950 align:middle line:84%
and seeing how these different
volatility estimates vary.

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:54.270 align:middle line:84%
So what this highlights is how
a choice of volatility measure

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:55.790 align:middle line:90%
matters.

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:59.190 align:middle line:84%
There can be significant
differences between them.

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:09.750 align:middle line:84%
And what would you anticipate is
a good property of a volatility

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:10.456 align:middle line:90%
estimator?

00:39:10.456 --> 00:39:13.950 align:middle line:90%


00:39:13.950 --> 00:39:19.910 align:middle line:84%
That's a very general
question, but intuitively,

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:22.680 align:middle line:84%
what would make one estimator
better than another?

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:30.990 align:middle line:90%


00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:35.510 align:middle line:84%
And there's no right or
wrong answer to this,

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:40.510 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's useful to
think about what you would like

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:47.210 align:middle line:84%
to have in an estimator, and
whether these estimators might

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:48.880 align:middle line:90%
be consistent with that or not.

00:39:48.880 --> 00:39:56.330 align:middle line:90%


00:39:56.330 --> 00:39:57.270 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:39:57.270 --> 00:40:03.090 align:middle line:90%


00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:07.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, one of the
problems, I guess

00:40:07.610 --> 00:40:12.370 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it a problem, with
the close to close estimator,

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:16.250 align:middle line:84%
is that if you have a rolling
window of close to close

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:22.050 align:middle line:84%
variance estimates, then if
you have a spike in returns,

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:24.610 align:middle line:84%
positive or negative,
that spike is

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:29.170 align:middle line:84%
going to remain in that
window until it falls off.

00:40:29.170 --> 00:40:36.210 align:middle line:84%
And so there can be elevated
volatilities due in the close

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:40.680 align:middle line:84%
to close series,
volatility series.

00:40:40.680 --> 00:40:47.580 align:middle line:84%
That may not be as persistent
with the other estimators.

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:50.660 align:middle line:84%
So when there are
spikes in volatility,

00:40:50.660 --> 00:40:53.800 align:middle line:84%
often those spikes
occur when there's

00:40:53.800 --> 00:41:00.560 align:middle line:84%
some great uncertainty in the
market that gets resolved.

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:06.280 align:middle line:84%
And the volatility often drops
a fair amount after that.

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:11.520 align:middle line:84%
And so I would think that
those volatility estimates

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:17.520 align:middle line:84%
that basically drop faster
following the spikes

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:20.400 align:middle line:90%
could be better.

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:24.960 align:middle line:84%
And certainly if we're trying
to use these volatility

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:30.600 align:middle line:84%
estimates to predict
future realized volatility,

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:34.800 align:middle line:90%
that property could be useful.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:40.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting in
section two of this case study

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:44.900 align:middle line:84%
is to just consider some
really, really basic time series

00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:49.480 align:middle line:84%
modeling of the different
volatility measures.

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:54.220 align:middle line:84%
So what we are modeling is
different volatility measures

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:58.980 align:middle line:84%
over a 21 day period that's
rolling through our time series.

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:05.100 align:middle line:84%
And so one of the most
basic forecasting models

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:08.700 align:middle line:84%
there is is a model
called the naive model.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:19.860 align:middle line:84%
And with a naive model,
we have a time series yt.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:30.500 align:middle line:84%
And we basically have that y
hat t is equal to yt minus 1.

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:38.570 align:middle line:84%
And if we think of yt is equal
to y hat t plus an error term,

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:46.930 align:middle line:84%
then our errors are equal to
yt minus y hat t, which is

00:42:46.930 --> 00:42:50.490 align:middle line:90%
equal to yt minus yt minus 1.

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:55.290 align:middle line:84%
And this specification
corresponds to a random walk

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:56.690 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:42:56.690 --> 00:43:00.530 align:middle line:84%
So there's basically a
random walk model for yt.

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:07.090 align:middle line:90%


00:43:07.090 --> 00:43:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And the random walk
model is consistent

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:14.450 align:middle line:84%
with efficient
markets where our best

00:43:14.450 --> 00:43:19.970 align:middle line:84%
estimate of what's going to
happen is where things are now.

00:43:19.970 --> 00:43:28.630 align:middle line:84%
So this naive model basically
has an autocorrelation function,

00:43:28.630 --> 00:43:31.370 align:middle line:90%
which has a very high--

00:43:31.370 --> 00:43:36.390 align:middle line:84%
or our data has a high
first order autocorrelation.

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:40.950 align:middle line:84%
And if we calculate
first differences, which

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:43.590 align:middle line:84%
are the residuals
from this model,

00:43:43.590 --> 00:43:51.070 align:middle line:84%
then we get this residual
diagnostic information, the time

00:43:51.070 --> 00:43:55.950 align:middle line:84%
series of residuals, the
autocorrelation function

00:43:55.950 --> 00:44:02.310 align:middle line:84%
of the residuals, and the
histogram of residuals.

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:06.190 align:middle line:84%
Now, would you say
that these residuals

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:13.040 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with white noise
that may be Gaussian or normal?

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:18.070 align:middle line:90%


00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:21.010 align:middle line:84%
I see some going mm-hmm
and others going hmm.

00:44:21.010 --> 00:44:24.110 align:middle line:90%


00:44:24.110 --> 00:44:28.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, one thing that's important
is that the autocorrelation

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:34.660 align:middle line:84%
function of residuals,
if the residuals are

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:36.380 align:middle line:84%
consistent with
white noise, there

00:44:36.380 --> 00:44:40.060 align:middle line:84%
should be no significant
autocorrelation at any lag.

00:44:40.060 --> 00:44:43.540 align:middle line:84%
And so there are some
spikes of autocorrelation

00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:46.100 align:middle line:84%
that break beyond
the critical levels.

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:51.140 align:middle line:84%
And so those are suggesting
that this is not white noise.

00:44:51.140 --> 00:44:56.740 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the
distribution of the residuals,

00:44:56.740 --> 00:45:00.700 align:middle line:84%
they're unimodal, but they
certainly aren't well described

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:02.246 align:middle line:90%
by a normal distribution.

00:45:02.246 --> 00:45:06.140 align:middle line:90%


00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:11.220 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:17.820 align:middle line:84%
So we can plot forecasts
of this naive method.

00:45:17.820 --> 00:45:21.040 align:middle line:90%
And then let's see.

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:27.700 align:middle line:84%
We can also fit an ARIMA
model to this close

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:30.730 align:middle line:90%
to close volatility series.

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:35.650 align:middle line:84%
And with the ARIMA
model, actually there's

00:45:35.650 --> 00:45:40.776 align:middle line:90%
this package in R called FPP2.

00:45:40.776 --> 00:45:45.570 align:middle line:90%


00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:51.290 align:middle line:84%
And this was created by the
authors of a book, Forecasting

00:45:51.290 --> 00:45:56.090 align:middle line:84%
Principles In Practice, that's
in the references to this.

00:45:56.090 --> 00:46:01.890 align:middle line:84%
They have an automated
ARIMA modeling function,

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:04.370 align:middle line:84%
which will take an
input, a time series,

00:46:04.370 --> 00:46:11.490 align:middle line:84%
as output, what is judged to
be a decent autoregressive

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:13.770 align:middle line:90%
integrated moving average model.

00:46:13.770 --> 00:46:28.022 align:middle line:84%
And so this ARIMA 310
corresponds to ARIMA p equals 3,

00:46:28.022 --> 00:46:34.990 align:middle line:84%
d equals 1, and
q equals 0, where

00:46:34.990 --> 00:46:37.510 align:middle line:84%
p is the order of
the autoregression.

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:38.990 align:middle line:90%
d is the order of differencing.

00:46:38.990 --> 00:46:39.950 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:46:39.950 --> 00:46:43.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How does
it [INAUDIBLE]?

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:47.230 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes,
it uses a criterion

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:52.550 align:middle line:84%
that's called the corrected
Akaike Information Criterion.

00:46:52.550 --> 00:47:01.870 align:middle line:84%
And so there's this AICC, which
is equal to the corrected AIC.

00:47:01.870 --> 00:47:06.350 align:middle line:84%
And the corrected AIC
is due to the fact

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:12.430 align:middle line:84%
that with shorter series,
there's a bias in the AIC

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:14.330 align:middle line:90%
that is systematic.

00:47:14.330 --> 00:47:18.030 align:middle line:84%
And so this correct
for that bias.

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:24.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we do this with
any fitted model, what's

00:47:24.110 --> 00:47:29.980 align:middle line:84%
really important to check is
how our estimated coefficients

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:33.340 align:middle line:84%
compare with their
standard errors.

00:47:33.340 --> 00:47:38.220 align:middle line:84%
When the signal estimate to
noise standard error ratio

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:41.380 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than 2, we
have some confidence

00:47:41.380 --> 00:47:44.620 align:middle line:84%
that that's an important
factor in the model.

00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:52.340 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, the AR3 has a
signal to noise ratio of almost

00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:58.820 align:middle line:84%
3, and the AR2 has a
ratio that's 1 and 1/2.

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:06.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we go
through, let's see,

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:09.260 align:middle line:84%
the second section
does the same analysis

00:48:09.260 --> 00:48:14.300 align:middle line:84%
of the Yang-Zhang
volatility estimate.

00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:28.640 align:middle line:84%
And I guess importantly here,
we have an ARIMA model now

00:48:28.640 --> 00:48:37.680 align:middle line:84%
for this Yang-Zhang model,
which is an ARIMA 111.

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:44.160 align:middle line:84%
And the coefficient
estimates are many multiples

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.380 align:middle line:90%
of the standard error.

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:47.920 align:middle line:90%
So these are highly significant.

00:48:47.920 --> 00:48:53.080 align:middle line:84%
And this is our
residual analysis check,

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:55.540 align:middle line:84%
which suggests that,
for the most part,

00:48:55.540 --> 00:48:58.660 align:middle line:84%
the residuals are
consistent with white noise.

00:48:58.660 --> 00:49:00.620 align:middle line:84%
The autocorrelations
tend to be close to 0.

00:49:00.620 --> 00:49:05.360 align:middle line:84%
However, there's this
large spike at 21.

00:49:05.360 --> 00:49:06.580 align:middle line:90%
And here the residuals.

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:10.273 align:middle line:84%
Now, why do you think
there's a large spike at 21?

00:49:10.273 --> 00:49:15.560 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.560 --> 00:49:20.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're computing
rolling statistics.

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:25.010 align:middle line:84%
And so the window
is of length 21.

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:30.310 align:middle line:84%
So this corresponds to
cases entering and exiting

00:49:30.310 --> 00:49:31.790 align:middle line:90%
the windows.

00:49:31.790 --> 00:49:36.470 align:middle line:84%
And so interestingly,
we can accommodate that

00:49:36.470 --> 00:49:39.870 align:middle line:84%
by fitting a
seasonal ARIMA model.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:43.590 align:middle line:84%
And so with the
seasonal ARIMA model

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:49.550 align:middle line:84%
I'll jump to the conclusion
here at the very end,

00:49:49.550 --> 00:49:58.990 align:middle line:84%
which is for the Yang-Zhang
volatility estimator.

00:49:58.990 --> 00:50:03.670 align:middle line:84%
This is its simple
autocorrelation function

00:50:03.670 --> 00:50:05.710 align:middle line:90%
before the model.

00:50:05.710 --> 00:50:11.110 align:middle line:84%
And then when we fit
the seasonal ARIMA model

00:50:11.110 --> 00:50:17.550 align:middle line:84%
automatically, it gives
us a 213 and a 201.

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:19.750 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:50:19.750 --> 00:50:29.730 align:middle line:84%
When we introduced you to time
series models before, let's see,

00:50:29.730 --> 00:50:42.930 align:middle line:84%
if we have an ARIMA
model, which is p, d, q,

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:51.650 align:middle line:84%
we basically have 1 minus phi
1 b minus phi 2 b squared minus

00:50:51.650 --> 00:51:02.890 align:middle line:84%
phi p b to the p power times yt
turns out to be equal to 1 minus

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:12.450 align:middle line:84%
theta 1b minus theta 2b
squared minus theta, or sorry,

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:23.160 align:middle line:84%
1 plus here, plus theta qb to
the q power times our error

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:23.820 align:middle line:90%
term.

00:51:23.820 --> 00:51:28.280 align:middle line:84%
So we have this kind of
mathematical equation

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:37.160 align:middle line:84%
for an ARIMA model, where
we assume that the epsilon t

00:51:37.160 --> 00:51:44.920 align:middle line:84%
series is white noise, which
means that its expectation is

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:53.040 align:middle line:84%
always 0 and the
variances are constant,

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:59.720 align:middle line:84%
and the correlation between any
different epsilons is equal to 0

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:04.280 align:middle line:90%
if t is not equal to t prime.

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:08.000 align:middle line:84%
The power of autoregressive
integrated moving average models

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:14.640 align:middle line:84%
is that these autoregressive
terms, as well as moving average

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:19.280 align:middle line:84%
terms, can account for
all of the correlations,

00:52:19.280 --> 00:52:22.020 align:middle line:84%
autocorrelations in
the original series.

00:52:22.020 --> 00:52:25.740 align:middle line:84%
And our residuals,
which are estimates

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:30.100 align:middle line:84%
of this error sequence, should
be consistent with white noise.

00:52:30.100 --> 00:52:33.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, what ends up
happening with--

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:39.110 align:middle line:90%


00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:42.660 align:middle line:84%
actually, I should
maybe put here yt prime,

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:49.060 align:middle line:84%
where y prime is maybe equal
to 1 minus b to the power d yt.

00:52:49.060 --> 00:52:54.980 align:middle line:84%
So if we have an
ordered d model,

00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:57.020 align:middle line:84%
we take the d-th
order difference

00:52:57.020 --> 00:53:00.020 align:middle line:90%
and put that into an ARMA model.

00:53:00.020 --> 00:53:07.020 align:middle line:84%
And if we have a
seasonal ARIMA model,

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:15.650 align:middle line:84%
we actually add a capital
P, capital D, capital Q.

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:19.950 align:middle line:84%
And here we have the
frequency of the data.

00:53:19.950 --> 00:53:26.850 align:middle line:84%
And in this example, I
considered a 21 period frequency

00:53:26.850 --> 00:53:31.730 align:middle line:84%
just to correspond
to the window size.

00:53:31.730 --> 00:53:42.200 align:middle line:84%
And what this model does is
it has another polynomial of--

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:55.450 align:middle line:90%


00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:59.810 align:middle line:90%
so this is b to the 2 times 21.

00:53:59.810 --> 00:54:07.130 align:middle line:84%
So we basically end up having
backshift operator polynomials

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:15.270 align:middle line:84%
that work with the seasonal
frequencies of the time series.

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:23.180 align:middle line:90%


00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:32.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is a polynomial of order
q of order p in B to the 12th--

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:35.640 align:middle line:90%
sorry, B to the 21.

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:40.100 align:middle line:84%
And then we would also
multiply this on this side,

00:54:40.100 --> 00:54:48.600 align:middle line:84%
perhaps, by a 1 minus
capital theta 1 B to the 21,

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:55.440 align:middle line:84%
or plus, plus theta 2
B to the 21 squared.

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:59.280 align:middle line:90%
So times 2.

00:54:59.280 --> 00:55:04.840 align:middle line:84%
And so this kind of
notation leads us

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:07.420 align:middle line:90%
to these coefficient estimates.

00:55:07.420 --> 00:55:11.900 align:middle line:84%
So there's the simple
order autoregressive

00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:13.470 align:middle line:90%
terms and moving average terms.

00:55:13.470 --> 00:55:16.180 align:middle line:90%


00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:20.660 align:middle line:84%
Order two, autoregressive,
order three, moving average.

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:24.220 align:middle line:84%
But then for the seasonal
autoregressive terms,

00:55:24.220 --> 00:55:27.680 align:middle line:90%
we have these terms here.

00:55:27.680 --> 00:55:29.560 align:middle line:84%
And for the seasonal
moving average,

00:55:29.560 --> 00:55:32.600 align:middle line:90%
we have the last term here.

00:55:32.600 --> 00:55:37.100 align:middle line:84%
And what's generally
important in fitting models is

00:55:37.100 --> 00:55:41.960 align:middle line:84%
when you consider the last
terms to enter into a model,

00:55:41.960 --> 00:55:48.000 align:middle line:84%
such as in this case, the
seasonal moving average term,

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:52.220 align:middle line:84%
its estimate is many multiples
of its standard error.

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:55.810 align:middle line:84%
So that's a very important
factor in the model.

00:55:55.810 --> 00:56:05.820 align:middle line:90%


00:56:05.820 --> 00:56:12.610 align:middle line:84%
As an anecdote,
let me just say--

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:13.490 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:56:13.490 --> 00:56:23.195 align:middle line:84%
If we take a series yt and we
look at a seasonal difference

00:56:23.195 --> 00:56:30.410 align:middle line:84%
operator and a first
order difference operator,

00:56:30.410 --> 00:56:33.930 align:middle line:84%
this turns out to be equal
to the first difference

00:56:33.930 --> 00:56:40.090 align:middle line:84%
operator multiplying the
seasonal difference operator.

00:56:40.090 --> 00:56:47.930 align:middle line:84%
And it equals basically 1
minus B plus B to the 21.

00:56:47.930 --> 00:56:55.130 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, minus B to the 21
plus B to the 22 times yt.

00:56:55.130 --> 00:57:02.530 align:middle line:84%
And so with seasonal differences
in autoregressive moving

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:07.430 align:middle line:84%
averages, whether we
take first differences

00:57:07.430 --> 00:57:11.310 align:middle line:84%
non-seasonally and then
seasonal differences

00:57:11.310 --> 00:57:13.330 align:middle line:84%
subsequently, or
reverse the order,

00:57:13.330 --> 00:57:16.160 align:middle line:84%
we end up getting the same
transformation of the data.

00:57:16.160 --> 00:57:19.350 align:middle line:90%


00:57:19.350 --> 00:57:21.450 align:middle line:84%
Well, actually with
economic time series,

00:57:21.450 --> 00:57:28.910 align:middle line:84%
where we have a seasonal
frequency of, say, months, then

00:57:28.910 --> 00:57:31.310 align:middle line:84%
basically the seasonal
differences correspond

00:57:31.310 --> 00:57:37.830 align:middle line:84%
to year over year changes,
whereas the simple differences

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:41.710 align:middle line:84%
are just period by
period differences.

00:57:41.710 --> 00:57:45.950 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, with this final
example here, this

00:57:45.950 --> 00:57:51.230 align:middle line:84%
is how the residual
analysis looks.

00:57:51.230 --> 00:57:55.750 align:middle line:84%
And it actually
does yield residuals

00:57:55.750 --> 00:57:58.030 align:middle line:84%
that do look a lot
like white noise,

00:57:58.030 --> 00:58:03.430 align:middle line:84%
although there is still
this persistent spike just

00:58:03.430 --> 00:58:06.800 align:middle line:84%
beyond the window width
for the computation.

00:58:06.800 --> 00:58:10.580 align:middle line:84%
But the distribution
of the residuals

00:58:10.580 --> 00:58:14.280 align:middle line:84%
looks more Gaussian,
perhaps, than, well,

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:17.540 align:middle line:90%
much more than the cases before.

00:58:17.540 --> 00:58:27.900 align:middle line:84%
So with that, in analyzing
different time series models,

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:31.100 align:middle line:84%
there are these
accuracy statistics

00:58:31.100 --> 00:58:35.380 align:middle line:84%
that can be computed for
any forecasting model.

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:39.420 align:middle line:84%
And so you're probably familiar
with root mean squared error

00:58:39.420 --> 00:58:42.220 align:middle line:90%
or mean squared error.

00:58:42.220 --> 00:58:47.380 align:middle line:84%
But we also have the MAPE, which
is the Mean Absolute Percentage

00:58:47.380 --> 00:58:48.540 align:middle line:90%
Error.

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:53.700 align:middle line:84%
And I actually find that
pretty useful for judging

00:58:53.700 --> 00:58:56.220 align:middle line:90%
how effective the models are.

00:58:56.220 --> 00:59:01.540 align:middle line:84%
And what's really nice
about the FPP2 package

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:06.080 align:middle line:84%
is that one can just
ask it to plot the time

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:10.960 align:middle line:84%
series and the forecast, and
it generates a point forecast

00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:13.800 align:middle line:84%
in a solid blue line
and prediction intervals

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:16.960 align:middle line:84%
about that forecast,
which assume

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:20.480 align:middle line:84%
Gaussian or normal distributions
for the error terms.

00:59:20.480 --> 00:59:25.400 align:middle line:84%
But one can see how those
evolve into the future.

00:59:25.400 --> 00:59:29.240 align:middle line:84%
And it's really consistent
for prediction intervals

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:32.560 align:middle line:84%
to get wider the
further out we forecast.

00:59:32.560 --> 00:59:33.780 align:middle line:90%
And that's happening here.

00:59:33.780 --> 00:59:38.960 align:middle line:84%
What's rather interesting
about this forecast

00:59:38.960 --> 00:59:44.720 align:middle line:84%
using the Yang-Zhang measure and
forecasting that into the future

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:49.380 align:middle line:84%
is that it actually, after
being at a lower level,

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:51.880 align:middle line:84%
is predicting that it
might go back to more

00:59:51.880 --> 00:59:54.560 align:middle line:90%
of a mean level of volatility.

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:56.810 align:middle line:84%
So that's an
interesting property.

00:59:56.810 --> 01:00:00.360 align:middle line:90%


01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:05.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's go back
to the notes here.

01:00:05.150 --> 01:00:08.230 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

01:00:08.230 --> 01:00:14.190 align:middle line:84%
There are extensions of
geometric Brownian motion

01:00:14.190 --> 01:00:18.870 align:middle line:90%
that we have seen before.

01:00:18.870 --> 01:00:23.150 align:middle line:84%
One of them is a Poisson
jump diffusion model,

01:00:23.150 --> 01:00:26.630 align:middle line:84%
where the stochastic
differential equation

01:00:26.630 --> 01:00:31.030 align:middle line:84%
has the Brownian motion
part as the first two terms,

01:00:31.030 --> 01:00:36.550 align:middle line:84%
but then has a jump
process as a last term

01:00:36.550 --> 01:00:43.490 align:middle line:84%
where the jump process can be
modeled as a Poisson process.

01:00:43.490 --> 01:00:50.710 align:middle line:84%
So a jump occurs
at some rate that

01:00:50.710 --> 01:00:53.310 align:middle line:90%
defines the Poisson process.

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:56.270 align:middle line:90%
And the rate is lambda.

01:00:56.270 --> 01:01:01.200 align:middle line:84%
And if an event in the
Poisson process occurs,

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:07.140 align:middle line:84%
then there's a jump
in the stock, which

01:01:07.140 --> 01:01:11.700 align:middle line:90%
is given by this factor here.

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:19.210 align:middle line:84%
So one can actually model data
with this Poisson jump diffusion

01:01:19.210 --> 01:01:19.710 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:22.740 align:middle line:90%


01:01:22.740 --> 01:01:29.820 align:middle line:84%
I ended up working with David
Pickard and Arshad Zakaria

01:01:29.820 --> 01:01:34.540 align:middle line:84%
on fitting this Poisson
jump diffusion model.

01:01:34.540 --> 01:01:39.860 align:middle line:84%
And it was an interesting
application in statistics

01:01:39.860 --> 01:01:44.380 align:middle line:90%
of using the EM algorithm.

01:01:44.380 --> 01:01:49.540 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the jumps are
treated as latent or hidden

01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:51.740 align:middle line:90%
values in our data.

01:01:51.740 --> 01:01:56.420 align:middle line:84%
And those hidden
values occur according

01:01:56.420 --> 01:01:59.400 align:middle line:84%
to Poisson probabilities,
like the probability

01:01:59.400 --> 01:02:03.320 align:middle line:84%
of one jump or two
jumps, can be computed

01:02:03.320 --> 01:02:05.800 align:middle line:90%
using the Poisson parameter.

01:02:05.800 --> 01:02:09.440 align:middle line:84%
And one can apply
this EM algorithm

01:02:09.440 --> 01:02:14.760 align:middle line:84%
to obtain maximum likelihood
estimates of all the parameters.

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:18.960 align:middle line:84%
And in applying this,
an interesting property

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:28.236 align:middle line:84%
that occurred was that
there were quite frequent--

01:02:28.236 --> 01:02:33.720 align:middle line:84%
or the rate of Poisson
events was quite high,

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:37.120 align:middle line:90%
like every couple of days.

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:42.840 align:middle line:84%
And what the model was
doing was capturing

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:46.800 align:middle line:84%
heavier tailed distributions
than a normal distribution.

01:02:46.800 --> 01:02:51.940 align:middle line:84%
So instead of a single
normal model for the return,

01:02:51.940 --> 01:02:56.190 align:middle line:84%
one has a mixture of a normal
with no jumps, a normal with one

01:02:56.190 --> 01:03:00.430 align:middle line:84%
jump, two jumps, weighted
according to Poisson rates.

01:03:00.430 --> 01:03:04.150 align:middle line:84%
And so the implication
of the model fitting

01:03:04.150 --> 01:03:08.070 align:middle line:84%
was that one didn't have
rare events necessarily

01:03:08.070 --> 01:03:17.070 align:middle line:84%
that occurred, but there was
evidence suggesting a jump

01:03:17.070 --> 01:03:21.510 align:middle line:84%
process that related
to trying to capture

01:03:21.510 --> 01:03:26.150 align:middle line:84%
the excess variability of
the stock price returns.

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:32.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, that leads to another
model extending Brownian motion.

01:03:32.810 --> 01:03:39.350 align:middle line:84%
And this is the Laplace
distribution, where if instead

01:03:39.350 --> 01:03:51.830 align:middle line:84%
of observing a time series over
equal increments, so between 0,

01:03:51.830 --> 01:03:55.530 align:middle line:84%
and one can think of just
equal increments of time.

01:03:55.530 --> 01:04:02.010 align:middle line:84%
So t1, t2, and so forth
up to tn, say, from time 0

01:04:02.010 --> 01:04:09.810 align:middle line:84%
to capital T. It may be that
we observe the Brownian motion

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:17.210 align:middle line:84%
process at random times
t1, and then here's t2,

01:04:17.210 --> 01:04:23.010 align:middle line:84%
here's t3, where the
increments of time

01:04:23.010 --> 01:04:25.490 align:middle line:90%
themselves are also random.

01:04:25.490 --> 01:04:32.930 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if the
times at which we observe

01:04:32.930 --> 01:04:41.410 align:middle line:84%
have a time increment that's
exponentially distributed,

01:04:41.410 --> 01:04:45.650 align:middle line:84%
then the increments
of the Brownian motion

01:04:45.650 --> 01:04:48.770 align:middle line:90%
follow a Laplace distribution.

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:54.520 align:middle line:84%
So heavier tailed distributions
than normal, and this

01:04:54.520 --> 01:05:01.608 align:middle line:84%
is the density of a
Laplace distribution,

01:05:01.608 --> 01:05:08.560 align:middle line:84%
it depends on the exponential
of minus the magnitude

01:05:08.560 --> 01:05:12.360 align:middle line:84%
of the deviation of
x from mu as opposed

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:15.560 align:middle line:90%
to the square of that deviation.

01:05:15.560 --> 01:05:17.320 align:middle line:84%
A Gaussian or
normal distribution

01:05:17.320 --> 01:05:22.180 align:middle line:84%
would have a power 2 there
with the Laplace distribution

01:05:22.180 --> 01:05:23.680 align:middle line:90%
as the absolute.

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:28.200 align:middle line:84%
And with this
distribution, we have

01:05:28.200 --> 01:05:34.080 align:middle line:84%
a mean mu, which is at the
center of the distribution.

01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:38.480 align:middle line:84%
The variance turns out to
depend on this scale factor

01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:43.720 align:middle line:90%
B, which is twice B squared.

01:05:43.720 --> 01:05:51.560 align:middle line:84%
So let's just take a look at
what Laplace models look like.

01:05:51.560 --> 01:05:56.420 align:middle line:84%
They basically are bilateral
exponential distributions.

01:05:56.420 --> 01:06:03.220 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider fitting the
daily returns on the S&P 500,

01:06:03.220 --> 01:06:08.420 align:middle line:84%
this is data for a
four year period.

01:06:08.420 --> 01:06:15.740 align:middle line:84%
This peaked distribution with
exponentially decaying density

01:06:15.740 --> 01:06:18.980 align:middle line:84%
is fitting the data
really quite well.

01:06:18.980 --> 01:06:24.180 align:middle line:84%
And more importantly, much
better really than a Gaussian

01:06:24.180 --> 01:06:25.580 align:middle line:90%
curve does.

01:06:25.580 --> 01:06:31.760 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have the fit of
the Gaussian bell curve in red,

01:06:31.760 --> 01:06:37.500 align:middle line:84%
comparing it to the
Laplace curve in blue.

01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:42.580 align:middle line:84%
And an interesting
question is, how do we

01:06:42.580 --> 01:06:48.450 align:middle line:84%
judge whether one model or
another is a good model?

01:06:48.450 --> 01:06:58.130 align:middle line:84%
And a useful tool is to
construct a q-q plot, which

01:06:58.130 --> 01:07:01.610 align:middle line:90%
is a quantile-quantile plot.

01:07:01.610 --> 01:07:08.930 align:middle line:84%
So we consider points where
the vertical value corresponds

01:07:08.930 --> 01:07:16.090 align:middle line:84%
to the magnitude of our data,
and the horizontal coordinate

01:07:16.090 --> 01:07:23.610 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to the theoretical
value for the smallest

01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:28.010 align:middle line:84%
from a sample of Laplace, the
second smallest from a given

01:07:28.010 --> 01:07:29.890 align:middle line:90%
Laplace distribution.

01:07:29.890 --> 01:07:35.050 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically
plot observed

01:07:35.050 --> 01:07:39.290 align:middle line:84%
quantiles versus theoretical
quantiles for the fitted model.

01:07:39.290 --> 01:07:45.290 align:middle line:84%
So this fits quite close to a
line, although it's not perfect.

01:07:45.290 --> 01:07:47.840 align:middle line:84%
At least if it were
perfect, it would even

01:07:47.840 --> 01:07:54.320 align:middle line:84%
snake around the straight line
where the sample quantiles equal

01:07:54.320 --> 01:07:56.200 align:middle line:90%
the theoretical quantiles.

01:07:56.200 --> 01:08:03.360 align:middle line:84%
If we do the same q-q plot for
a Gaussian or normal model,

01:08:03.360 --> 01:08:06.280 align:middle line:84%
this is what our
q-q plot looks like.

01:08:06.280 --> 01:08:11.920 align:middle line:84%
And so one can see that this
second q-q plot is not fitting

01:08:11.920 --> 01:08:15.640 align:middle line:90%
the data very well at all.

01:08:15.640 --> 01:08:24.760 align:middle line:84%
So this would favor using the
Laplace instead of the Gaussian

01:08:24.760 --> 01:08:29.240 align:middle line:84%
as a model for
independent daily returns.

01:08:29.240 --> 01:08:34.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, the independence of
our period by period returns

01:08:34.640 --> 01:08:39.380 align:middle line:84%
is one that may not be
satisfied by the data.

01:08:39.380 --> 01:08:44.859 align:middle line:84%
And Robert Engle
proposed looking

01:08:44.859 --> 01:08:52.899 align:middle line:84%
at time series of returns and
considering the possibility

01:08:52.899 --> 01:08:56.060 align:middle line:84%
that the variability
of these returns

01:08:56.060 --> 01:09:00.899 align:middle line:84%
given by the variability
of the epsilon t terms,

01:09:00.899 --> 01:09:08.740 align:middle line:84%
that these, in fact, have time
varying volatility sigma t,

01:09:08.740 --> 01:09:17.420 align:middle line:84%
where the sigma t squared
evolves according to a moving

01:09:17.420 --> 01:09:21.620 align:middle line:90%
average of past squared errors.

01:09:21.620 --> 01:09:27.220 align:middle line:84%
So an autoregressive
conditionally heteroskedastic

01:09:27.220 --> 01:09:31.580 align:middle line:90%
model is what he proposed.

01:09:31.580 --> 01:09:37.620 align:middle line:84%
And in looking at
this model, it's

01:09:37.620 --> 01:09:47.290 align:middle line:84%
interesting to note that if
one can re-express the ARCH

01:09:47.290 --> 01:09:55.010 align:middle line:84%
model in terms of this
squared error term

01:09:55.010 --> 01:10:01.290 align:middle line:84%
and have this model in
the second equation being

01:10:01.290 --> 01:10:05.450 align:middle line:84%
consistent with the first,
and this second model

01:10:05.450 --> 01:10:11.970 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to there being
an autoregressive model

01:10:11.970 --> 01:10:15.250 align:middle line:84%
consistent with the
squared returns.

01:10:15.250 --> 01:10:19.650 align:middle line:84%
And so this Lagrange
multiplier test

01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:23.850 align:middle line:84%
can fit the autoregressive model
to these epsilon hat squareds

01:10:23.850 --> 01:10:28.250 align:middle line:84%
and test whether there is
autoregressive structure or not.

01:10:28.250 --> 01:10:32.490 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the
autocorrelations of the S&P 500

01:10:32.490 --> 01:10:41.390 align:middle line:84%
returns squared, you can
see that the first almost 10

01:10:41.390 --> 01:10:45.870 align:middle line:84%
autocorrelations are
significantly different from 0

01:10:45.870 --> 01:10:47.150 align:middle line:90%
in magnitude.

01:10:47.150 --> 01:10:52.190 align:middle line:84%
So there is strong time
dependence in the log returns.

01:10:52.190 --> 01:10:56.490 align:middle line:84%
And so how does
one fit this model?

01:10:56.490 --> 01:11:03.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, the go to typically
in statistical modeling

01:11:03.750 --> 01:11:07.750 align:middle line:84%
is to consider a
likelihood approach

01:11:07.750 --> 01:11:15.130 align:middle line:84%
where we consider a density for
our data specified by the model.

01:11:15.130 --> 01:11:23.110 align:middle line:84%
So if the error is epsilon
t are independent Gaussian

01:11:23.110 --> 01:11:26.110 align:middle line:84%
distributions with
different variances,

01:11:26.110 --> 01:11:33.910 align:middle line:84%
then this likelihood function
defines the joint density

01:11:33.910 --> 01:11:37.340 align:middle line:84%
of our data given
underlying parameters.

01:11:37.340 --> 01:11:44.060 align:middle line:84%
And what becomes tricky with
this likelihood specification

01:11:44.060 --> 01:11:49.540 align:middle line:84%
is that there, in fact, are
constraints on our parameters.

01:11:49.540 --> 01:11:55.380 align:middle line:84%
So with the ARCH model, we
can't have a negative alpha,

01:11:55.380 --> 01:11:59.420 align:middle line:84%
because with the
Gaussian squared epsilon,

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:05.100 align:middle line:84%
that could be arbitrarily large
negative, or epsilon could be--

01:12:05.100 --> 01:12:06.600 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

01:12:06.600 --> 01:12:09.580 align:middle line:84%
If the alphas were
negative, then the epsilon

01:12:09.580 --> 01:12:11.780 align:middle line:84%
squared could be
arbitrarily large positive

01:12:11.780 --> 01:12:13.600 align:middle line:84%
and we'd get a
negative variance.

01:12:13.600 --> 01:12:15.340 align:middle line:90%
So we can't have that.

01:12:15.340 --> 01:12:19.500 align:middle line:84%
Also we have this property of
the sum of the autoregressive

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:21.900 align:middle line:90%
parameters is less than 1.

01:12:21.900 --> 01:12:24.180 align:middle line:84%
And this corresponds
to the process

01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:29.940 align:middle line:84%
not being explosive
in its movements.

01:12:29.940 --> 01:12:32.820 align:middle line:90%
And then let's see.

01:12:32.820 --> 01:12:40.160 align:middle line:84%
An extension of the ARCH
models was done by Bollerslev.

01:12:40.160 --> 01:12:46.920 align:middle line:84%
And this basically adds
to our ARCH model terms,

01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:49.800 align:middle line:90%
like an order p ARCH model.

01:12:49.800 --> 01:12:57.560 align:middle line:84%
It adds an order q set of terms
on lags of the volatility.

01:12:57.560 --> 01:13:03.400 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider the special
case of the GARCH 1, 1 model,

01:13:03.400 --> 01:13:09.000 align:middle line:84%
this is its form, which is
very simple and parsimonious.

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:14.080 align:middle line:84%
What's rather surprising is
that the empirical specification

01:13:14.080 --> 01:13:19.040 align:middle line:84%
of these models is such
that the GARCH 1, 1

01:13:19.040 --> 01:13:22.520 align:middle line:84%
model is often
chosen or identified

01:13:22.520 --> 01:13:26.320 align:middle line:90%
as the best GARCH order case.

01:13:26.320 --> 01:13:29.560 align:middle line:84%
So we end up having
a very simple model

01:13:29.560 --> 01:13:34.370 align:middle line:84%
with just a few parameters to
characterize the volatility.

01:13:34.370 --> 01:13:39.330 align:middle line:90%


01:13:39.330 --> 01:13:43.510 align:middle line:84%
These notes talk about how the
GARCH model induces an ARMA

01:13:43.510 --> 01:13:44.170 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:13:44.170 --> 01:13:46.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is some
time series results

01:13:46.510 --> 01:13:48.390 align:middle line:90%
that are gone through here.

01:13:48.390 --> 01:13:54.990 align:middle line:84%
And there's a long run
variance of the GARCH model

01:13:54.990 --> 01:13:57.970 align:middle line:90%
that is given here.

01:13:57.970 --> 01:14:02.830 align:middle line:84%
And with a GARCH 1, 1
model, there basically

01:14:02.830 --> 01:14:13.550 align:middle line:84%
is a long run volatility
sigma star, which

01:14:13.550 --> 01:14:15.240 align:middle line:90%
has this particular formula.

01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:24.230 align:middle line:90%


01:14:24.230 --> 01:14:28.150 align:middle line:84%
So one can find the long run
variance of a GARCH model

01:14:28.150 --> 01:14:30.630 align:middle line:90%
with this formula.

01:14:30.630 --> 01:14:34.490 align:middle line:84%
This formula also leads
to updates of volatility

01:14:34.490 --> 01:14:38.110 align:middle line:84%
being a multiple of
the long run variance,

01:14:38.110 --> 01:14:41.760 align:middle line:84%
plus a deviation from
that long run variance.

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:48.110 align:middle line:90%


01:14:48.110 --> 01:14:52.130 align:middle line:84%
So we have a maximum likelihood
estimation for these models.

01:14:52.130 --> 01:14:57.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, I wanted to
share with you a case

01:14:57.250 --> 01:15:01.730 align:middle line:84%
study using ARCH and GARCH
models with foreign exchange

01:15:01.730 --> 01:15:03.370 align:middle line:90%
returns.

01:15:03.370 --> 01:15:07.930 align:middle line:84%
And so this note
uses data collected

01:15:07.930 --> 01:15:15.490 align:middle line:84%
from the Federal Reserve
on foreign exchange values.

01:15:15.490 --> 01:15:19.810 align:middle line:84%
Section 1.2 actually
fits Brownian motion

01:15:19.810 --> 01:15:21.990 align:middle line:90%
using different time scales.

01:15:21.990 --> 01:15:24.290 align:middle line:90%
So not assuming time dependence.

01:15:24.290 --> 01:15:29.880 align:middle line:84%
It actually is interesting
to think about different time

01:15:29.880 --> 01:15:32.480 align:middle line:90%
scales.

01:15:32.480 --> 01:15:39.400 align:middle line:84%
But if we go to
section 1.3 and look

01:15:39.400 --> 01:15:41.900 align:middle line:84%
at time dependence
in squared returns,

01:15:41.900 --> 01:15:48.840 align:middle line:84%
this is the dollar
pound returns.

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:54.120 align:middle line:84%
This shows the autocorrelation
and partial autocorrelation

01:15:54.120 --> 01:15:57.120 align:middle line:90%
of the returns.

01:15:57.120 --> 01:16:02.040 align:middle line:84%
So there's strong
partial autocorrelations

01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:03.720 align:middle line:90%
of the actual series.

01:16:03.720 --> 01:16:07.680 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at
the squared returns,

01:16:07.680 --> 01:16:12.780 align:middle line:84%
the actual series has high
autocorrelations over time.

01:16:12.780 --> 01:16:17.720 align:middle line:84%
But the partial autocorrelations
have very high spikes

01:16:17.720 --> 01:16:18.780 align:middle line:90%
at low lags.

01:16:18.780 --> 01:16:21.320 align:middle line:84%
So this suggests an
autoregressive model

01:16:21.320 --> 01:16:23.680 align:middle line:90%
in the squared returns.

01:16:23.680 --> 01:16:29.630 align:middle line:84%
And the note fits an
ARCH 1 and ARCH 2.

01:16:29.630 --> 01:16:34.070 align:middle line:84%
So one lag, or sorry,
one autoregressive

01:16:34.070 --> 01:16:37.470 align:middle line:84%
term, two autoregressive
terms or 10,

01:16:37.470 --> 01:16:40.470 align:middle line:90%
and then fits the GARCH 1, 1.

01:16:40.470 --> 01:16:44.630 align:middle line:84%
And the results are
highlighted here,

01:16:44.630 --> 01:16:46.870 align:middle line:84%
just showing you the different
coefficient estimates

01:16:46.870 --> 01:16:48.790 align:middle line:90%
for these different models.

01:16:48.790 --> 01:16:51.110 align:middle line:84%
They're all
statistically significant

01:16:51.110 --> 01:16:53.670 align:middle line:90%
in terms of the parameters.

01:16:53.670 --> 01:16:56.030 align:middle line:84%
What's of interest,
though, is just

01:16:56.030 --> 01:17:01.230 align:middle line:84%
to look at the time series plot
of the fitted volatilities using

01:17:01.230 --> 01:17:03.330 align:middle line:90%
ARCH of different orders.

01:17:03.330 --> 01:17:06.990 align:middle line:90%
So here's the ARCH 1 model.

01:17:06.990 --> 01:17:10.710 align:middle line:90%
Here's the ARCH 2 model.

01:17:10.710 --> 01:17:15.490 align:middle line:84%
A property of these ARCH models
is that it never goes below 0.

01:17:15.490 --> 01:17:17.830 align:middle line:90%
There's a lower bound.

01:17:17.830 --> 01:17:21.830 align:middle line:84%
Here's the ARCH 10 model, which
looks like it's maybe tracking

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:23.370 align:middle line:90%
time varying volatility well.

01:17:23.370 --> 01:17:30.650 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, we can compare
that with the GARCH 1, 1 model.

01:17:30.650 --> 01:17:34.490 align:middle line:84%
So instead of having 10
autoregressive parameters

01:17:34.490 --> 01:17:38.650 align:middle line:84%
in ARCH, the
generalized ARCH model

01:17:38.650 --> 01:17:42.530 align:middle line:84%
is capturing time varying
volatility in an appealing

01:17:42.530 --> 01:17:47.650 align:middle line:84%
way, whether that's really
valid or not is of interest.

01:17:47.650 --> 01:17:50.770 align:middle line:84%
But it certainly is an
alternative to these arch models

01:17:50.770 --> 01:17:52.480 align:middle line:90%
that is quite powerful.

01:17:52.480 --> 01:17:56.850 align:middle line:90%


01:17:56.850 --> 01:18:01.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's just a graph
of all three together.

01:18:01.490 --> 01:18:05.610 align:middle line:84%
This note actually
does a final section

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:11.570 align:middle line:84%
of looking at what happens if
our error distribution is not

01:18:11.570 --> 01:18:15.396 align:middle line:84%
Gaussian, but is
a t distribution.

01:18:15.396 --> 01:18:18.170 align:middle line:90%


01:18:18.170 --> 01:18:21.170 align:middle line:84%
There are packages,
rugarch, which

01:18:21.170 --> 01:18:27.960 align:middle line:84%
allow us to specify parameters
and fits using t models,

01:18:27.960 --> 01:18:30.320 align:middle line:84%
t distribution models
for the errors.

01:18:30.320 --> 01:18:37.200 align:middle line:84%
And what's very
curious is in judging

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:47.160 align:middle line:84%
the histogram of
standardized residuals,

01:18:47.160 --> 01:18:51.680 align:middle line:84%
the t distribution seems
to do a decent job.

01:18:51.680 --> 01:18:59.040 align:middle line:84%
And so here's with Gaussian
residuals, I guess.

01:18:59.040 --> 01:19:06.000 align:middle line:84%
And then here's the
Gaussian residuals and q-q

01:19:06.000 --> 01:19:10.280 align:middle line:84%
plot associated with the
standardized Gaussian

01:19:10.280 --> 01:19:11.960 align:middle line:90%
assumption.

01:19:11.960 --> 01:19:18.400 align:middle line:84%
Then finally, there's the q-q
plot of standardized residuals

01:19:18.400 --> 01:19:20.780 align:middle line:90%
with a t distribution assumed.

01:19:20.780 --> 01:19:25.540 align:middle line:84%
So what's significant is just
how the quantile-quantile plot

01:19:25.540 --> 01:19:27.580 align:middle line:84%
suggests that this
model is really fitting

01:19:27.580 --> 01:19:30.380 align:middle line:84%
the data much, much better
in terms of characterizing

01:19:30.380 --> 01:19:31.900 align:middle line:90%
the variation.

01:19:31.900 --> 01:19:36.860 align:middle line:84%
And the choice of
which order model

01:19:36.860 --> 01:19:45.900 align:middle line:84%
to use for the
degrees of freedom,

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:52.300 align:middle line:84%
there's this negative
log likelihood function

01:19:52.300 --> 01:19:56.000 align:middle line:84%
of models for different
t distributions

01:19:56.000 --> 01:19:59.300 align:middle line:84%
as we vary the degrees
of freedom for the t.

01:19:59.300 --> 01:20:02.500 align:middle line:84%
And the t distribution
has a heavier tail

01:20:02.500 --> 01:20:05.500 align:middle line:90%
than a Gaussian distribution.

01:20:05.500 --> 01:20:07.960 align:middle line:84%
At least if the degrees
of freedom is really high,

01:20:07.960 --> 01:20:10.700 align:middle line:84%
it's virtually
equivalent to a Gaussian.

01:20:10.700 --> 01:20:15.100 align:middle line:84%
But this is suggesting
that there's possibly a 9

01:20:15.100 --> 01:20:18.020 align:middle line:90%
degrees of freedom for that.

01:20:18.020 --> 01:20:20.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's a very
interesting finding.

01:20:20.290 --> 01:20:25.890 align:middle line:84%
And it actually arises with
some mathematical analysis

01:20:25.890 --> 01:20:34.210 align:middle line:84%
that t distributions are
appropriate if we think

01:20:34.210 --> 01:20:39.370 align:middle line:84%
of spherical symmetry in
the number of dimensions

01:20:39.370 --> 01:20:42.610 align:middle line:90%
equal to the degree of freedom.

01:20:42.610 --> 01:20:48.250 align:middle line:84%
And so if you imagine a
joint distribution that

01:20:48.250 --> 01:20:52.090 align:middle line:84%
is spherically symmetric in
really high dimensions, when you

01:20:52.090 --> 01:20:55.590 align:middle line:84%
project that down
onto coordinate axes,

01:20:55.590 --> 01:20:58.250 align:middle line:84%
you end up getting a
Gaussian distribution.

01:20:58.250 --> 01:21:07.010 align:middle line:84%
But if their symmetry is just
of, say, 9 dimensions, then

01:21:07.010 --> 01:21:09.090 align:middle line:84%
you end up having
a t distribution

01:21:09.090 --> 01:21:11.610 align:middle line:90%
for what those projections are.

01:21:11.610 --> 01:21:18.630 align:middle line:84%
So this suggests that
in terms of time periods

01:21:18.630 --> 01:21:25.890 align:middle line:84%
over which the distribution
of volatility is stable,

01:21:25.890 --> 01:21:31.190 align:middle line:90%
could be close to 9 dimensions.

01:21:31.190 --> 01:21:35.870 align:middle line:84%
So perhaps corresponding to
9 periods of data, I believe.

01:21:35.870 --> 01:21:36.370 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:21:36.370 --> 01:21:37.412 align:middle line:90%
Well, we'll finish there.

01:21:37.412 --> 01:21:39.350 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
interesting material

01:21:39.350 --> 01:21:42.710 align:middle line:84%
for you to check
out, if you like.

01:21:42.710 --> 01:21:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And I hope you can enjoy reading
some of the articles of people

01:21:48.150 --> 01:21:51.110 align:middle line:84%
who've studied
volatility and see what

01:21:51.110 --> 01:21:53.770 align:middle line:90%
their work has contributed.

01:21:53.770 --> 01:21:56.550 align:middle line:84%
And it's a really
interesting area

01:21:56.550 --> 01:22:01.750 align:middle line:84%
for final papers,
modeling volatility,

01:22:01.750 --> 01:22:03.050 align:middle line:90%
predicting volatility.

01:22:03.050 --> 01:22:06.010 align:middle line:84%
This is really scratching
the surface of the topic.

01:22:06.010 --> 01:22:09.770 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of interesting
problems you could study.

01:22:09.770 --> 01:22:10.270 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:22:10.270 --> 01:22:12.220 align:middle line:90%
Thanks a lot.

01:22:12.220 --> 01:22:18.000 align:middle line:90%